;;   gmap's `f` expected `(-> Int64 (GIter (GIter Int64)))` (over-nested 1 GIter) vs closure's correct `(-> Int64 (GIter Int64))`.
;; TRUE SOURCE (6 sessions, evidence-backed): gmap's MEMOIZED scheme is over-nested because `param_types[f]` is set
;;   over-deep in the recursive-param BODY solve (solve_recursive_params → collect_param_constraints over
;;   `GIter.Cons(f(h), gmap(rest,f))`). Watched-var trace: f's var binds `(GIter _)` CORRECTLY; the INNER element var
;;   over-binds to `(GIter Int64)` — the recursive-generic element tie (f-result-elem = gmap-result-elem = self-call
;;   elem) accumulates ONE extra GIter. FIX DIRECTION: the body solve must not let f's result element absorb gmap's
;;   OWN container `(GIter …)` wrapper. NEEDS: a holistic re-read of collect_param_constraints's recursive-sum element
;;   tie (the inner-var over-bind is one indirection past incremental probes) — a bounded pairing pass.
;; RULED OUT (do NOT re-explore): call-site seed (correct), apply_scheme_to_args closure re-solve arm (solved correct),
;;   solved_lambda_arrow_under, all arg_ty_in_env arms, Cons-tail unify (types Any), f(h)-fresh, payload_ty_at_
;;   instantiation (victim of over-nested scrut_ty), the apply_scheme_to_args per-arg loop (scheme already over-nested).
;; NON-BLOCKING: v-iterators' real generic adapters are DIRECT producers (already covered); this is the exotic
;;   closure-RETURNS-a-producer-CALL case. Full session-by-session trace is in the ;; blocks after the repro.
